/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (De Zerbi is known for employing strategies that invite high press from the opponents, only to then exploit the spaces created by such a press. This approach creates "artificial transitions". The concept based on a form of 'trap' or 'bait'. In the build-up phase, De Zerbi's team would circulate the ball in a way that tempts the opponents to press high and leave their defensive line. Once the opponent commits to the press, the team quickly transitions into an attack, using speed and precise passing to bypass the press and exploit the spaces left in the opponent's half. This tactic creates numerical superiority in the final third because the opponents have less time to transition back to a defensive shape, having committed players forward for the press. )
/E (De Zerbi is known for employing strategies that invite high press from the opponents, only to then exploit the spaces created by such a press. This approach creates "artificial transitions". The concept based on a form of 'trap' or 'bait'. In the build-up phase, De Zerbi's team would circulate the ball in a way that tempts the opponents to press high and leave their defensive line. Once the opponent commits to the press, the team quickly transitions into an attack, using speed and precise passing to bypass the press and exploit the spaces left in the opponent's half. This tactic creates numerical superiority in the final third because the opponents have less time to transition back to a defensive shape, having committed players forward for the press. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
