/P 1400 0 R
/E (First Play Through the Middle: The central areas of the pitch are critical because they offer the most direct path towards the opponent's goal and provide the best angles for spreading the ball to all parts of the field. By initiating play through the middle, De Zerbi's teams can effectively engage the maximum number of players in the game, facilitating better control and creating opportunities to penetrate the opposition's defensive line. The double pivot and center-backs are crucial for this. They work together to find space and pass the ball between each other, drawing the opposition in and creating gaps to exploit further up the pitch.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
566 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1401 0 R
/E (Play Around if Opposition Focus Their Press Centrally: When the opposition concentrates their press in the central areas, it often leaves flanks less guarded. Playing around this central press exploits these weaker spots, allowing the team to advance the ball into less contested areas, which can lead to crossing opportunities or diagonal balls back into the center from a more advantageous position.The center-backs and pivots need to be adept at quickly switching the play. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
567 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1402 0 R
/E (Play Back to Attract Pressure: Playing the ball backwards is a tactical ploy to draw the opposition further up the field, thereby increasing the space behind them. This can be particularly effective against high-pressing teams, as it tempts them out of their defensive structure, creating opportunities for quick breaks through fast, forward passes into the space created. The center-backs and the goalkeeper are often involved in this tactic, circulating the ball among themselves and occasionally back to the goalkeeper to encourage the opposition to press higher, thus opening up space elsewhere.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
568 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1403 0 R
/E (Play Into the Direction Where the Pressure Comes From: This principle uses the momentum of the pressing team against them. By playing into the direction of the press, De Zerbi's team can exploit the brief moments when pressing players are out of position, having committed to a certain direction. This creates opportunities to bypass multiple layers of the press with a single well-timed and directed pass. This requires high technical skill and calm under pressure, as the players must make accurate, quick passes back towards and then immediately away from the pressing threat. The players must also anticipate the press effectively, knowing beforehand where the subsequent pass should go.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
