/P 1404 0 R
/E (Vertical Staggering and Space Creation: To create vertical channels that can be exploited for progressive, attacking play. Staggering players vertically helps in pulling opposing players out of position, thereby creating space to operate within the midfield and attacking third. In De Zerbi's system, the double pivot adjusts their positioning based on the ball's location and the opposition's structure. One might drop deeper to receive the ball from the center-backs, while the other positions slightly higher, ready to link up with the attackers. This vertical staggering ensures that there are always multiple options for the player in possession, whether to recycle the ball or progress it forward.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
582 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1405 0 R
/E (Vertical Attacking Combinations: The "S-Combination" starts with a center back who plays the ball to the first pivot. This pivot then executes a short pass to the second pivot. The fluid movement and passing continue with the second pivot turning to play an incisive pass to one of the two attacking midfielders positioned higher up the field. This series of passes forms a quick, zigzagging movement pattern, resembling the letter "S". This combination is effective in breaking lines, especially in tight spaces. It allows the team to quickly shift the point of attack from a deep, central area to more advanced positions, exploiting gaps in the opponent's midfield and defensive lines. The quick, short passing disrupts the opponent's shape and draws them out, creating spaces for attacking midfielders or forwards to exploit.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
583 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1406 0 R
/E (Utilizing Diamond Structures in Wide Areas: When central areas are congested, shifting the build-up to wide areas can help in maintaining the flow of the game. Creating diamond structures on the flanks utilizes the full-backs and wingers effectively, providing width and additional passing options. As the hourglass shape naturally draws the opposition inward, it leaves spaces on the flanks that can be exploited. The full-backs, who are crucial in this setup, move up to form diamonds with the nearest central midfielder, winger, and forward or attacking midfielder. This structure not only provides stability but also options for back-passes, crosses, or cut-backs into the box.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
